#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203707 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203885 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "004a102d-cfb9-539a-9fdd-11ca604fd7ec" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Transitive Relations"}] :question/id #uuid "c802160b-40ba-5bef-a32b-010ebdc4851f" :question/math-academy-id "e-14665" :question/problem "Consider the following relation defined on the set $A = \\{\\heartsuit,\\spadesuit,\\diamondsuit\\}$.\n$R = \\big\\{(\\heartsuit,\\heartsuit), (\\heartsuit,\\spadesuit), (\\heartsuit,\\diamondsuit), (\\spadesuit,\\spadesuit), (\\spadesuit,\\diamondsuit), (\\diamondsuit, \\diamondsuit) \\big\\}$\nDetermine whether the relation $R$ is transitive or not." :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z \\in A$, we have\n$(x,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,z) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (x,z) \\in R$.\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with\n\n$$\nx \\neq y\n$$\n\nand $(y,z)$ with\n\n$$\ny \\neq z{:}\n$$\n\n- Consider the pair $(\\heartsuit,\\spadesuit)$ whose second component is $\\spadesuit$. It can be linked with the following pair from $R$ whose first component is $\\spadesuit{:}$ Linking $(\\heartsuit,\\spadesuit)$ with $(\\spadesuit,\\diamondsuit)$ requires that $(\\heartsuit,\\diamondsuit) \\in R$, and this pair is in $R$. $\\:\\:{\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\n- Consider the pair $(\\heartsuit,\\diamondsuit)$ whose second component is $\\diamondsuit$. It can't be linked with any pair from $R$ since none of them have a first component of $\\diamondsuit$ and a second component other than $\\diamondsuit$.\n- Consider the pair $(\\spadesuit,\\diamondsuit)$ whose second component is $\\diamondsuit$. It can't be linked with any pair from $R$ since none of them have a first component of $\\diamondsuit$ and a second component other than $\\diamondsuit$.\n\nSo, for all $x,y,z \\in A$, we have\n$(x,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,z) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (x,z) \\in R$.\n\nTherefore, the relation is transitive."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203708 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203887 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f65dc026-1ec7-51f3-abc2-4a8ce95f9c2c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Transitive Relations From a Digraph"}] :question/id #uuid "7a9c02ab-eae3-52ee-9dd4-c81ca204ad4d" :question/math-academy-id "e-14666" :question/problem "Which of the following digraphs represents a transitive relation defined on the set $A = \\{1,2,3\\}$?\n\n![](images/f4/f4ad23f3c7794456a4cc01d4f801bfe4217605c5e17f1f612c5955be6bcaf6e6.png)" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z \\in A$, we have\n$(x,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,z) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (x,z) \\in R$.\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with\n\n$$\nx \\neq y\n$$\n\nand $(y,z)$ with\n\n$$\ny \\neq z\n$$\n\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have \"linked\" arrows $x \\rightarrow y$ and $y \\rightarrow z$, then the arrow $x \\rightarrow z$ must be in the digraph too.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our digraphs.\n\n- Digraph I represents a transitive relation. There are no pairs of vertices for which $x \\rightarrow y$ and $y \\rightarrow z$.\n- Digraph II does not represent a transitive relation. It has arrows $1 \\rightarrow 2$ and $2 \\rightarrow 1$, but has no arrow $1 \\rightarrow 1$.\n- Digraph III represents a transitive relation. For any pair of arrows $x \\rightarrow y$ and $y \\rightarrow z$, the arrow $x \\rightarrow z$ also belongs to the digraph.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203709 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203888 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "947f058c-4c47-52df-8c28-aed016d2b148" :knowledge-point/title "Transitive Relations Defined On Infinite Sets"}] :question/id #uuid "9e7589ef-e172-57ee-84f3-e085724db718" :question/math-academy-id "e-14667" :question/problem "Which of the following relations are transitive?\n\n- $x \\: R \\: y$ if and only if $x \\not\\mid y$, defined on $\\mathbb{Z}$\n- $x \\: S \\: y$ if and only if $x = y$, defined on $\\mathbb{R}$\n- $x \\: T \\: y$ if and only if $6 \\mid (x+y)$, defined on $\\mathbb{Z}$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z \\in A$, we have\n$(x,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,z) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (x,z) \\in R$.\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with\n\n$$\nx \\neq y\n$$\n\nand $(y,z)$ with\n\n$$\ny \\neq z\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations.\n\n- Relation $R$ is not transitive. Notice that $(2,3) \\in R$ since $2 \\not\\mid 3$, and$(3,4) \\in R$ since $3 \\not\\mid 4$, but$(2,4) \\notin R$ since $2 \\mid 4$.\n- Relation $S$ is transitive. Indeed, if $(x,y) \\in S$ and $(y,x) \\in S$, then $(x,z) \\in S$. Notice that\n$x = y \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad y = z \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x = z$\nfor all $x,y,z \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n- Relation $T$ is not transitive. Notice that $(2,4) \\in T$ since $6 \\mid (2+4)$, and$(4,8) \\in T$ since $6 \\mid (4+8)$, but$(2,8) \\notin T$ since $6 \\not\\mid (2+8)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$S$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203718 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203886 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "630cd12d-1141-51ae-8f2e-a881622868e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Transitive Relations in Special Cases"}] :question/id #uuid "b2641213-6efe-5412-a585-9cb348ab00b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-14701" :question/problem "Which of the following relations on the set $A = \\{\\alpha, \\beta, \\delta\\}$ are transitive?\n\n1. $R_1 = A^2$\n2. $R_2 = \\{(\\alpha,\\beta), (\\alpha,\\delta)\\}$\n3. $R_3 = \\{(\\beta,\\alpha), (\\beta,\\delta), (\\delta, \\beta)\\}$\n4. $R_4 = \\{(\\alpha,\\alpha), (\\beta,\\beta), (\\delta, \\delta)\\}$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z \\in A$, we have\n$(x,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,z) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (x,z) \\in R$.\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with\n\n$$\nx \\neq y\n$$\n\nand $(y,z)$ with\n\n$$\ny \\neq z{:}\n$$\n\nMoreover, we recall the following:\n\n- The universal relation $R = A^2$ is transitive since it includes all possible ordered pairs.\n- The empty relation $R = \\emptyset$ is vacuously transitive since the antecedent in the implication is always false, and therefore the implication is always true.\n- A relation containing no \"linked\" pairs is always transitive.\n- A relation containing only reflexive pairs is always transitive.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations:\n\n- $R_1$ is transitive since it's the universal relation. $\\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n- $R_2$ is transitive since it contains no \"linked\" pairs. $\\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n- $R_3$ is *not* transitive. Linking $(\\beta,\\delta)$ with $(\\delta, \\beta)$ requires that $(\\beta,\\beta) \\in R_3$. However, this pair is *not* in $R_3$. $\\:\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n- $R_4$ is transitive since it contains only reflexive pairs. $\\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204265 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203885 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "004a102d-cfb9-539a-9fdd-11ca604fd7ec" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Transitive Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8db7bf4d-d3c4-53a3-8dbb-a05dcd66941c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∉" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200334} {:answer/id #uuid "a7a9b443-4a0f-5c11-b29e-cbebd10c3360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∈ \\mid" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200335}] :answer-field/id #uuid "78e8fcde-d428-5235-9232-052e39dd78e0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200333} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f021ab7-85bd-5ac3-82ae-5fb57ed17592" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(z, x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200337} {:answer/id #uuid "8f3490b0-f474-5c62-8185-84944591c576" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x, z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200338} {:answer/id #uuid "3cc46807-7b92-5dec-b704-45bbca1bb50c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(z, z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200339} {:answer/id #uuid "40b2556d-153f-5137-84c5-85d1932456d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y, y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200340} {:answer/id #uuid "5de47036-b04d-5d83-9d51-7a53016fab47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x, x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200341}] :answer-field/id #uuid "769d8967-73be-58fe-8da3-d1dfbef3a35d" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/selec